          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "The combination of burning during urination, a temperature, and back pain can be concerning, and it is recommended to seek immediate medical attention. These symptoms may indicate a serious infection, such as a urinary tract infection (UTI) or potentially something more severe like sepsis, especially if accompanied by other alarming signs (e.g., very high or low temperature, confusion, rapid breathing). According to guidance on sepsis, it’s crucial to call emergency services if there are indications of a serious infection (source: \"Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection\").\n\nFor non-emergency cases related to urinary tract infections, guidance suggests that uncomplicated UTIs typically improve within 48 hours with appropriate treatment. If symptoms do not improve, reassessment is necessary (source: \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\"). Possible treatments may include antibiotics, but specific medication details and dosages are outside the scope of this guidance.\n\nPlease consult a healthcare professional for personalized advice and evaluation of your symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
